{
  "title": "Accessory Navicular",
  "url": "https://www.orthobullets.com/pediatrics/4070/accessory-navicular",
  "subtitle": "Updated: Feb 21 2026",
  "sections": [
    {
      "text": "summary",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "3",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Acessory Navicular is a common idiopathic condition of the foot that presents with an enlargement of the navicular bone.",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": []
        },
        {
          "text": "Diagnosis is made with plain radiographs of the foot showing a plantar medial enlargement of the navicular bone.",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "5",
          "children": []
        },
        {
          "text": "Treatment is generally conservative with shoe modifications and a short period of cast immobilization in patients with symptoms. Surgical excision is indicted for patients with progressive pain that have failed extended nonoperative management.",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "4",
          "children": []
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Epidemiology",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "3",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Incidence",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "accessory navicular is a normal variant seen in up to 12% of population",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "majority of patients are asymptomatic",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Demographics",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "more commonly symptomatic in females",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Etiology",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "3",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Pathophysiology",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "pathoanatomy",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "occurs as a **plantar medial enlargement** of the navicular bone",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "4",
                  "children": []
                },
                {
                  "text": "exists as accessory bone or as completely ossified extension of the navicular",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "3",
                  "children": []
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Genetics",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "inheritance pattern",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "autosomal dominant",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "3",
                  "children": []
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Associated conditions",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "flat feet",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "2",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "posterior tibial tendon insufficiency",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "2",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Anatomy",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "3",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Osteology",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "4",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "navicular bone normally has a single center of ossification",
              "table": null,
              "images": [
                {
                  "src": "https://upload.orthobullets.com/topic/4070/images/navpm.jpg",
                  "caption": ""
                },
                {
                  "src": "https://upload.orthobullets.com/topic/4070/images/navsup.jpg",
                  "caption": ""
                }
              ],
              "importance": "5",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "ossifies at age 3 in girls and 5 in boys and fuses at 13 years of age",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "4",
                  "children": []
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "text": "an accessory navicular is a normal variant from which the tuberosity of the navicular develops from a secondary ossification center that fails to unite during childhood",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "1",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "the accessory navicular does not begin to ossify prior to age 8",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "2",
                  "children": []
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Muscles",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "tibialis posterior inserts onto the tuberosity (medial) of the navicular bone",
              "table": null,
              "images": [
                {
                  "src": "https://upload.orthobullets.com/topic/4070/images/navitp.jpg",
                  "caption": ""
                }
              ],
              "importance": "4",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "innervated by tibial nerve",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "5",
                  "children": []
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Ligament",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "plantar calcaneonavicular (spring) ligament originates from sustentaculum tali and inserts on to navicular",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "plantar support for head of talus",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "3",
                  "children": []
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "text": "bifurcate ligament attaches the anterior process of the calcaneus to the navicular and cuboid bones",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "lateral support",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "3",
                  "children": []
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "text": "dorsal talonavicular ligament connects the neck of the talus to the dorsal surface of the navicular bone",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "dorsal support",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "3",
                  "children": []
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Blood Supply",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "dorsalis pedis artery (dorsal aspect)",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "medial plantar artery (plantar aspect)",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "anastomosis between dorsalis pedis and medial plantar arteries (medial surface of tuberosity)",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Classification",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "3",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "",
          "table": [
            {
              "header": true,
              "cells": [
                {
                  "colspan": 3,
                  "bullets": [
                    {
                      "text": "**Radiographic Classification**",
